    "growth_divergence_warning": "Equity 5yr CAGR (2.8%) diverges by far more than 30% from EPS 5yr CAGR (16.2%) and revenue 5yr CAGR (8.5%): UHS has retired ~25% of its share count since FY2020 ($5.5B of buybacks FY20-H1-26), so book value understates the compounding. Revenue CAGR is selected as the anchor, and the base path decelerates from 7% well below it.",
    "notes": "Owner-FCF (reported FCF - SBC) CAGRs are uninformative: FY2020 FCF ($1.63B) is inflated by CARES Act Medicare advance payments that reversed in FY2021 (owner FCF -$46M), and FY2022 ($177M) is the repayment/labour-cost trough. SBC grew roughly in line with revenue (FY20 $65.8M -> FY25 $95.7M), so the owner-FCF and reported-FCF series do not diverge for SBC reasons; the 3yr gap (62% vs 48%) is a small-base effect off the FY22 trough. EPS CAGR is inflated by both margin recovery from the FY22 trough and buybacks."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "Volumes recover only modestly from the cut 2026 guidance (acute admissions +1.5-2.5%, behavioral patient days +1-2%), with revenue growth carried by pricing, acuity and the 2025-26 capacity additions (Palm Beach Gardens, 178 acute and 264 behavioral beds). Medicaid supplemental-payment pressure from the OBBBA provider-fee phase-down and higher PL/GL reserves roughly offset operating leverage, holding pre-SBC EBITDA margin near 15% mid-cycle. Capex normalises to ~5% of revenue after the construction cycle, lifting owner FCF from ~$0.75B toward ~$1.3B by the early 2030s.",
    "bull": "Behavioral health bed scarcity lets UHS keep pricing ahead of labour inflation while new de novo hospitals and freestanding EDs ramp to mature margins; Medicaid cuts are phased slowly or offset by state directed-payment programs; acute admissions return to 3%+ as the ACA-subsidy coverage shock fades. Margin rebuilds toward the FY2017-19 ~16% level and capex drops below the long-run average, converting into materially higher FCF that funds the buyback.",
    "bear": "OBBBA work requirements and provider-fee threshold cuts take Medicaid supplemental payments out of both segments from FY2028, ACA subsidy expiry raises uninsured/self-pay mix and bad debt, and behavioral-health litigation (the $535M verdict, Illinois abuse suits) brings higher liability cost and licensing/regulatory friction in the higher-margin segment. Acute volumes keep migrating to outpatient. Margin slides to 13% -- near the FY2022-23 trough -- while capex stays elevated to defend share."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "Hospital operator valued on unlevered owner FCF (SBC expensed, share count flat, debt and NCI deducted once in the bridge). Value is driven by the mid-cycle EBITDA margin and capex normalisation; reimbursement policy is the dominant risk and is carried in the scenario margins and a 25% bear weight rather than in the discount rate. Terminal value capped at 12-15x FCF, below Gordon at an 8% WACC, consistent with the regulated, labour-intensive, policy-exposed character of US hospitals.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Medicaid supplemental-payment cuts under OBBBA (provider-fee thresholds 6% -> 3.5% FFY2028-32) and Medicaid work requirements",
      "Behavioral-health litigation: $535M jury verdict and further abuse/neglect suits; uninsured portion not deducted in the bridge",
      "Labour cost inflation and PL/GL claim severity (2026 reserves +~$50M)",
      "Acute volume migration to outpatient settings; 2026 volume guidance already cut",
      "Capex not normalising after the build cycle would keep FCF near FY25 levels"
    ]
  },
